","nucleus_padj":0.119205,"nucleus_log2fc":-0.0965308,"nucleus_score":-1.98183,"pct_ad":0.342529,"pct_control":0.357155,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.999342,"donor_log2fc":-0.130986,"donor_lfcse":0.0916092,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":-1.63485e-05,"degas_log2fc":-0.307369,"degas_padj":6.20715e-11},{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_symbol":"GABRA3","gene_symbol_current":"GABRA3","gene_id":"ENSG00000011677","nucleus_tested":false,"nucleus_significant":null,"nucleus_direction":"not tested","nucleus_padj":null,"nucleus_log2fc":null,"nucleus_score":null,"pct_ad":null,"pct_control":null,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.885609,"donor_log2fc":-0.136051,"donor_lfcse":0.165181,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":-0.00550858,"degas_log2fc":null,"degas_padj":null}],"modules":[{"cell_type":"Ex","gene_id":"ENSG00000011677","module":"blue","kme":0.247446,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.391996,"module_significant":false}],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000011677","entrez_id":["2556"],"uniprot":["P34903"],"ncbi_summary":"GABA is the major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the mammalian brain where it acts at GABA-A receptors, which are ligand-gated chloride channels. Chloride conductance of these channels can be modulated by agents such as benzodiazepines that bind to the GABA-A receptor. At least 16 distinct subunits of GABA-A receptors have been identified. [provided by RefSeq, Jul 2008].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"P34903","text":"Alpha subunit of the heteropentameric ligand-gated chloride channel gated by gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA), a major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the brain (PubMed:16412217, PubMed:29053855). GABA-gated chloride channels, also named GABA(A) receptors (GABAAR), consist of five subunits arranged around a central pore and contain GABA active binding site(s) located at the alpha and beta subunit interface(s) (By similarity). When activated by GABA, GABAARs selectively allow the flow of chloride anions across the cell membrane down their electrochemical gradient (PubMed:16412217, PubMed:29053855). Chloride influx into the postsynaptic neuron following GABAAR opening decreases the neuron ability to generate a new action potential, thereby reducing nerve transmission (PubMed:16412217, PubMed:29053855)."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_04","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}